From e4faeb1dc001747e88907cba862060b66b89f1a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 14:35:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Point the augmentation tests at the vendored augmentation
 module

"Replace albumentations with a vendored augmentation module" removed
`albumentations` from the package but left the three test modules that import
it, so against that patch series the tests do not collect at all:

    ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'albumentations'
    ERROR tests/pose_estimation_pytorch/data/test_preprocessor.py
    ERROR tests/pose_estimation_pytorch/data/test_transforms.py
    ERROR tests/pose_estimation_pytorch/other/test_dataset.py

That is 29 tests, and they are the only ones that exercise the replacement:
they build `A.Compose` pipelines and feed them through `Preprocessor`,
`PoseDataset` and DeepLabCut's own `transforms.py` wrappers.

`deeplabcut.pose_estimation_pytorch.data.augmentation` is a drop-in for the
part of the albumentations API DeepLabCut uses, so the change is the import
line and nothing else. All 29 then pass, with no behavioural difference to
account for. They are not passing vacuously either: disabling the image
resize in the vendored `Resize.apply` fails three of them.

Their coverage is narrower than it looks, though. They assert shapes, so
disabling `Resize.apply_to_keypoints` and `apply_to_bboxes`, or
`Normalize.apply`, leaves all 29 green -- and nothing else covers those. The
vendored-module patch adds no tests of its own, and the sdist's
`tests/test_dataset_augmentation.py` is about the imgaug-based TensorFlow
augmenters, not this module. A transform-by-transform comparison against
albumentations is still owed.

These three modules are not in the PyPI sdist, so this patch applies to the
upstream git tree the recipe fetches as a second source rather than to the
sdist it builds.  `test_dataset.py` is not run there: it resolves the
openfield example project relative to the installed package, which in a
conda environment is `site-packages`, and it draws its parameters from
`random.randint` at collection time.
